# The version IRI above is a Trusty URI, module RA. To check it: take the
# triples of this file EXCLUDING the build-stamp block (:software, :plan,
# :src_*, :dep*), write them as N-Triples, replace the artifact code in the
# version IRI with a single space, sort the lines, join with newlines, add a
# trailing newline, and SHA-256 it. Base64url of that digest, unpadded, is the
# 43 characters that follow the module identifier. The full artifact code,
# module and hash together, is:
#
#   RAwXcRPIIonfnlZoP_K9_GGLt934vTVAmqWNY0cJUtZ4Y
#
# The same triples are published as dist/usps-states.nt, which is that exact
# serialisation with the code substituted back in. Hash THOSE BYTES.
#
# Do not re-derive them by loading this file into an RDF library and asking
# it to write N-Triples. Some libraries normalise literals on the way out
# and hand back a different graph: rdflib 7.6.0 rewrites an xsd:dateTime of
# "...Z" as "...+00:00", which is the same instant, a different RDF literal,
# and a different hash. Z is the canonical form per XML Schema Part 2, so
# this file keeps it. That is the cost of hashing a serialisation, stated
# out loud rather than discovered by whoever checks first.
#
# That .nt file is RDFC-1.0 canonical form — the W3C Recommendation of
# 2024-05-21, formerly URDNA2015 — and not merely a recipe of our own. It
# can be sorted rather than canonicalised only because this graph contains
# no blank nodes, which is why the checksums above are named resources
# instead. A test fails the build if one ever comes back.
